===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-|id=speak}}
{{inh+|en|enm|bannen||to summon; to banish; to curse}}, partly from {{inh|en|ang|bannan|t=to summon, command, proclaim, call out}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*bannan}}; and partly from {{der|en|non|banna|t=to prohibit; to curse}}, both from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*bannaną||to proclaim, to order; to summon; to ban; to curse, forbid}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro||*bʰh₂-new-ti ~ bʰh₂-n̥w-énti}}, innovative nasal-infixed zero-grade athematic present of {{m|ine-pro|*bʰeh₂-||to say}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|bannen||to ban, exile, discard}}, {{cog|de|bannen||to exile, to exorcise, captivate, excommunicate}}, {{cog|sv|banna||to ban, scold}}, {{cog|sa-ved|भनति|tr=bhánati}}, {{cog|hy|բան}} and perhaps {{cog|sq|banoj||to reside, dwell}}. See also {{m|en|banal}}, {{m|en|abandon}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[summon]]; to [[call out]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[anathematize]]; to [[pronounce]] an ecclesiastical [[curse]] upon; to place under a ban.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[curse]]; to [[execrate]].
#* '''c. 1555''', {{w|Hugh Latimer}}, ''a sermon'':
#*: {{quote|en|They will curse and '''ban'''{{...}}even into the deep pit of hell, all that gainsay their appetite.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[prohibit]]; to [[interdict]]; to [[proscribe]]; to [[forbid]] or block from [[participation]].
#: {{ux|en|Bare feet are '''banned''' in this establishment.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1816|author=w:Lord Byron|title=[[s:The Works of Lord Byron (ed. Coleridge, Prothero)/Poetry/Volume 4/The Prisoner of Chillon|The Prisoner of Chillon]]
|passage=To whom the goodly earth and air Are '''banned'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=14-12-2011|author=Steven Morris|work=w:The Guardian
|title=[http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2011/dec/14/woman-killed-kitten-microwave-jail Devon woman jailed for 168 days for killing kitten in microwave]| passage=Jailing her on Wednesday, magistrate Liz Clyne told Robins: "You have shown little remorse either for the death of the kitten or the trauma to your former friend Sarah Knutton." She was also '''banned''' from keeping animals for 10 years.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-08-10|volume=408|issue=8848|magazine=w:The Economist|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/leaders/21583270-new-zealands-plan-regulate-designer-drugs-better-trying-ban-them-and-failing-new A new prescription]| passage=No sooner has a [synthetic] drug been blacklisted than chemists adjust their recipe and start churning out a subtly different one. These &ldquo;legal highs&rdquo; are sold for the few months it takes the authorities to identify and '''ban''' them, and then the cycle begins again.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Soumya Karlamangla|title=California Bans All Plastic Bags After Its First Effort Backfired|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/09/23/us/california-plastic-bags-ban.html|date=23-09-2024|passage=Gov. Gavin Newsom signed legislation on Sunday '''banning''' the sale at grocery checkouts of all plastic bags, regardless of thickness.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To curse; to [[utter]] curses or [[malediction]]s.
#* {{RQ:Scott Waverley|passage=:“I seldom '''ban''', sir,” said he to the man; “but if you play any of your hound's-foot tricks, and leave puir Berwick before he's sorted, to rin after spuilzie, deil be wi' me if I do not give your craig a thraw”}}

